is  \n\nFor each candidate \\(L_{0}\\) we let \\(R = T_{3}\\setminus L_{0}\\) (the points not covered by\n\\(L_{0}\\)). The two remaining lines must be sunny, i.e. each must join a pair of points whose\nconnecting line is **not** vertical, horizontal, nor of slope \\(-1\\). We examine all six\npossibilities.\n\n1. **\\(L_{0}=x=1\\)** (covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\)).  \n   \\(R=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   All three possible pairs are vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\); none yields a sunny\n   line. Impossible.\n\n2. **\\(L_{0}=y=1\\)** (covers \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\)).  \n   \\(R=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \n   Again every pair is vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\). Impossible.\n\n3. **\\(L_{0}=x+y=4\\)** (covers \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\)).  \n   \\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   All pairs are non\u2011sunny. Impossible.\n\n4. **\\(L_{0}=x=2\\)** (covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\)).  \n   \\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   The only sunny pair among these points is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n   The remaining two points \\((1,1),(1,3)\\) are vertical, a non\u2011sunny line. Impossible.\n\n5. **\\(L_{0}=y=2\\)** (covers \\((1,2),(2,2)\\)).  \n   \\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   The only sunny pair is \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n   The leftover points \\((1,1),(3,1)\\) are horizontal, non\u2011sunny. Impossible.\n\n6. **\\(L_{0}=x+y=3\\)** (covers \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)).  \n   \\(R=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   The only sunny pair is \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n   The remaining points \\((1,3),(3,1)\\) have slope \\(-1\\), a non\u2011sunny line. Impossible.\n\nIn every possible choice for the non\u2011sunny line, the four remaining points cannot be split\ninto two sunny pairs. Hence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nFor \\(n=3\\) there is no set of three distinct lines satisfyin