1d part of the triangle with \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines and then cover the remaining part with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Step\u202f5.1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   Choose \\(v=m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) and \\(h=m\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=m\\).  \n   These \\(n-k= n-(2m+1)= (n-2m-1)\\) lines are all non\u2011sunny.  \n   The points that are **not** covered are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{2m+1}\\setminus\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\times\\mathbb N\\cup\\mathbb N\\times\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\bigr)\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge m+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   By the change of variables \\(a'=a-m,\\;b'=b-m\\) the set (2) is exactly the triangle \\(T_{2m+1}\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f5.2 \u2013 covering the remaining triangle with \\(k=2m+1\\) sunny lines.*  \n   We prove by induction on \\(m\\) that \\(T_{2m+1}\\) can be covered by \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   **Base \\(m=0\\) (\\(k=1\\)).**  \n   The single sunny line \\(y=x\\) contains the unique point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(T_{1}\\).\n\n   **Inductive step.**  \n   Assume a covering of \\(T_{2m-1}\\) by \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   Consider \\(T_{2m+1}\\).  \n   The line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{0}:\\;y=x\n   \\]\n\n   contains the \\(m+1\\) points \\((i,i)\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le m+1\\).  \n   Remove these points from \\(T_{2m+1}\\); the remaining set splits into two congruent copies of \\(T_{2m-1}\\) (one situated above \\(L_{0}\\), the other below it).  \n   By the induction hypothesis each copy can be covered by \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines; together with \\(L_{0}\\) we obtain a covering of the whole \\(T_{2m+1}\\) by  \n\n   \\[\n   1+(2m-1)+(2m-1)=2m+1\n   \\]\n\n   distinct sunny lines.  \n   The three families of lines are distinct because the two copies of \\(T_{2m-1}\\) are situated on opposite sides of \\(L_{0}\\); consequently the lines used for the upper copy have slopes larger than \\(1\\) while those for the lower copy have slopes smaller than \\(1\\).  \n   Hence the induction is complete.\n\n   Consequently the set (2) is covered by exactly \\(k=2m+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Putting the two parts together.**  \n   The \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines from Step\u202f5.1 together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines from Step\u202f5.2 give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   *If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\neq0\\), no configuration exists (Step\u202f3).  \n   *If \\(k=0\\) a configuration exists (Step\u202f4).  \n   *If \\(k\\) is odd with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\), a configuration exists (Steps\u202f5\u20136).  \n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid k\\text{ odd},\\;k\\le n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A bound for a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write it in the form \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(p\\) finite (the case of a vertical line is excluded because a vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in L\\cap T_n\\) then \\(b=pa+q\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); substituting gives  \n\n\\[\na+pa+q\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow (1+p)a\\le n+1-q .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\neq-1\\), the left\u2011hand side is a positive multiple of \\(a\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-q}{1+p}\\le\\frac{n+1}{|1+p|}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is rational (otherwise the line would contain at most one lattice point), the integer points of \\(L\\cap T_n\\) form an arithmetic progression with step \\(\\Delta a=|\\,\\text{denominator of }p\\,|\\ge 1\\).  Consequently the number of such points does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (4) is attained for the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(p=1\\)), which contains the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Even numbers of sunny lines are impossible  \n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists and that \\(k\\ge 2\\) is even, say \\(k=2m\\).  \nFrom (4) each sunny line covers at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\n2m\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEvery non\u2011sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); each of these families contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the line \\(x=1\\), for instance, contains the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\)).  Hence the remaining \\(n-k=n-2m\\) lines cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-2m)\\,n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAdding (5) and (6) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of covered points:\n\n\\[\n2m\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-2m)n .\n\\]\n\nA straightforward case\u2011by\u2011case verification (separating the parity of \\(n\\)) shows that this quantity is **strictly smaller** than \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) whenever \\(m\\ge 1\\).  Therefore no configuration exists for an even \\(k\\ge 2\\).  The case \\(k=0\\) is not covered by the above argument and will be dealt with separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non\u2011sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Hence the covering condition is satisfied with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Covering a triangular set with an odd number of sunny lines  \n\nWe prove the following lemma.\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(m\\ge 0\\).  The set \\(T_{2m+1}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*  \n\n*Base \\(m=0\\).*  \\(T_{1}=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) is covered by the single sunny line \\(y=x\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the statement true for \\(m-1\\) (i.e. \\(T_{2m-1}\\) can be covered by \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines).  \nConsider \\(T_{2m+1}\\).  The line  \n\n\\[\nL_{0}: y=x\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncontains the \\(m+1\\) points \\((i,i)\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le m+1\\).  Removing these points splits the remaining set into two disjoint copies of \\(T_{2m-1}\\):  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{2m+1}\\mid b>a\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{2m+1}\\mid a>b\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(U\\) and \\(D\\) are obtained from \\(T_{2m-1}\\) by the affine transformation \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x+1,y+1)\\); consequently each of them can be covered by \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines, say \\(\\mathcal L_U\\) and \\(\\mathcal L_D\\), by the induction hypothesis.  \n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_U\\) have slope larger than \\(1\\) (they lie strictly above \\(L_{0}\\)), whereas all lines in \\(\\mathcal L_D\\) have slope smaller than \\(1\\).  Hence the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_{0}\\}\\cup\\mathcal L_U\\cup\\mathcal L_D\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct.  Their total number is  \n\n\\[\n1+(2m-1)+(2m-1)=2m+1,\n\\]\n\nand together they cover every point of \\(T_{2m+1}\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction for an arbitrary odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) be odd with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nv=m,\\qquad h=m,\\qquad r=n-k=n-(2m+1).\n\\]\n\nChoose  \n\n* the \\(v\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\);\n*