            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Reduction.**  \n   Choose the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \n   The points that are still uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the same triangular set but of size \\(k\\).  \n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to\n\n   > **(★)**  *For a given integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) determine whether the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.*\n\n2. **A universal upper bound for a sunny line.**  \n   Let a sunny line be written as \\(y=px+q\\) with \\(p\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(p\\neq\\infty\\).  \n   Write \\(p=\\dfrac{u}{v}\\) in lowest terms (\\(u,v\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;v>0,\\; \\gcd(u,v)=1\\)).  \n   Integer points of the line are exactly the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=(a_0+tv,\\;b_0+tu),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is any integer point on the line.  \n   For two consecutive points (\\(t\\) and \\(t+1\\)) the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta (a+b)= (v+u).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the line is sunny we have \\(u\\neq0\\) and \\(u\\neq -v\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |v+u|\\ge 2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, if \\(|v+u|=1\\) then either \\(u= -v\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) or \\(u=0\\) (horizontal), both forbidden.)\n\n   Inside the triangle \\(T_k\\) the possible values of the sum \\(a+b\\) are the integers\n   \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\); there are exactly \\(k\\) different values.  \n   By (1)–(2) a sunny line can meet at most one point for each admissible value of the sum, and the distance between two successive admissible sums is at least \\(2\\).  \n   Hence a sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_k\\).  Equality in (3) is attained for the diagonal line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).\n\n3. **Necessary condition for (★).**  \n   Let a family of sunny lines cover \\(T_k\\).  \n   By (3) each line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points, while \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \n   Therefore the number \\(s\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\;\\ge\\;\n   \\frac{|T_k|}{\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor}\n   =\\begin{cases}\n        k      &\\text{if }k\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n        k+1    &\\text{if }k\\text{ is even}.\n     \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently, for an even \\(k\\) at least \\(k+1\\) sunny lines are necessary; with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines the covering is impossible.  \n   For an odd \\(k\\) the lower bound is \\(k\\); we shall show that it is attainable.\n\n4. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge 0\\)).  \n   We exhibit \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(T_{2m+1}\\).\n\n   *Diagonal line*  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;y=x .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   It contains the \\(m+1\\) points \\((i,i)\\;(1\\le i\\le m+1)\\).\n\n   *Upper family.*  \n   For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   U_j:\\; y = (j+1)x + (m+1-j).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the slope \\(j+1\\) is a positive integer \\(\\neq0\\), each \\(U_j\\) is sunny.  \n   For a fixed \\(j\\) the integer points of \\(U_j\\) inside \\(T_{2m+1}\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\, (a,b)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,m+1-j,\\;\n          b=(j+1)a+m+1-j \\,\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   One checks that for every pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b>a\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) there is a unique \\(j\\) such that \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(U_j\\).  \n   Moreover the sets \\(\\{U_j\\cap T_{2m+1}\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n   *Lower family.*  \n   For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{j+1}\\,x + (2m+2)-\\frac{j}{j+1}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{j+1}\\) is different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\); hence each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.  \n   The integer points of \\(L_j\\) inside \\(T_{2m+1}\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{\\, (a,b)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,m+1-j,\\;\n          b= -\\frac{a}{j+1}+2m+2-\\frac{j}{j+1}\\,\\bigr\\},\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the points with \\(a>b\\).  As before the families \\(\\{L_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together they contain every point of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) with \\(a>b\\).\n\n   The three families \\(\\{D\\},\\{U_j\\},\\{L_j\\}\\) are mutually disjoint because every line of the upper family has slope \\(>1\\), every line of the lower family has slope \\(<0\\), while the diagonal has slope \\(1\\).  \n   Counting the lines we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\text{diagonal})\\;+\\;m\\;(\\text{upper})\\;+\\;m\\;(\\text{lower})\\;=\\;2m+1=k\n   \\]\n\n   distinct sunny lines, and by the description above they cover **all** points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   * If \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all of them are non‑