            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular set.**  \n   Use \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   They cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \n   The still uncovered points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the same triangular set but of size \\(k\\).  \n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to  \n\n   > **(★)**  *For a given integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) decide whether the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.*\n\n2. **A universal upper bound for a sunny line.**  \n   Write a sunny line in reduced form  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{u}{v}x+q ,\\qquad u,v\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;v>0,\\;\\gcd(u,v)=1,\n   \\]\n\n   with \\(u\\neq0\\) (not horizontal) and \\(u\\neq -v\\) (not slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   All integer points on the line are  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=(a_0+tv,\\;b_0+tu),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is any integer point on the line.  \n   The sum \\(s(t)=a+b\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   s(t)=s(0)+t(u+v).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(u\\neq0\\) and \\(u\\neq -v\\) we have \\(|u+v|\\ge 2\\).  \n   (Indeed, if \\(|u+v|=1\\) then \\((u,v)=(\\pm1,0)\\) or \\((0,\\pm1)\\) or \\((u,-u\\pm1)\\); the first two give a forbidden horizontal or vertical line, the third gives slope \\(-1\\).)  \n\n   Inside the triangle \\(T_k\\) the possible values of \\(s=a+b\\) are the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\qquad(\\text{exactly }k\\text{ different values}).\n   \\]\n\n   By (2) the values taken by a sunny line form an arithmetic progression with difference \\(|u+v|\\ge 2\\); consequently the line can meet at most one point for each admissible value of the sum, and the number of admissible values it can meet is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{|u+v|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **any sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_k\\).**\n\n3. **Necessary condition for (★).**  \n   The set \\(T_k\\) has  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  If \\(s\\) sunny lines cover \\(T_k\\), (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{|T_k|}{\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor}\n   =\\begin{cases}\n        k      &\\text{if }k\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n        k+1    &\\text{if }k\\text{ is even}.\n     \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore for an **even** \\(k\\) at least \\(k+1\\) sunny lines are required; with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines the covering is impossible.  \n   For an **odd** \\(k\\) the lower bound is \\(k\\); we shall now exhibit a construction attaining it.\n\n4. **Inductive construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  \n   We shall produce \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(T_{2m+1}\\).\n\n   *Base case \\(m=0\\) (\\(k=1\\)).*  \n   The single line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) covers the unique point \\((1,1)\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{2m+1}\\).  \n   Consider \\(T_{2(m+1)+1}=T_{2m+3}\\).  \n   The new points are those with \\(a+b=2m+3\\) (the outermost diagonal) together with the points having \\(a+b=2m+2\\) that were not present in \\(T_{2m+1}\\).  \n   We add **two** new sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L^{+}_{m+1}&:\\; y=-(m+2)\\,x+(m+2)(2m+3),\\\\[2mm]\n   L^{-}_{m+1}&:\\; y=-\\frac{1}{m+2}\\,x+\\Bigl(2m+3-\\frac{m+1}{m+2}\\Bigr),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   and keep all the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) unchanged.  \n\n   *Verification of the two new lines.*  \n\n   1. **\\(L^{+}_{m+1}\\).**  \n      For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m+2\\) we have  \n\n      \\[\n      \\bigl(a,b\\bigr)=\\bigl(t,\\;-(m+2)t+(m+2)(2m+3)\\bigr)\n      =\\bigl(t,\\;2m+3-(m+1)t\\bigr).\n      \\]\n\n      Because \\(1\\le t\\le m+2\\) we have \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\); moreover  \n\n      \\[\n      a+b=t+2m+3-(m+1)t=2m+3-(m)t\\le2m+3,\n      \\]\n\n      so \\((a,b)\\in T_{2m+3}\\).  \n      The \\(m+2\\) points obtained for \\(t=1,\\dots ,m+2\\) are **exactly** the points of \\(T_{2m+3}\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) equals \\(2m+3\\) (the outer diagonal).  \n      Hence \\(L^{+}_{m+1}\\) covers the whole new outer diagonal.\n\n   2. **\\(L^{-}_{m+1}\\).**  \n      Write the line as  \n\n      \\[\n      y=-\\frac{1}{m+2}x+\\frac{(m+2)(2m+3)- (m+1)}{m+2}.\n      \\]\n\n      For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m+1\\) set  \n\n      \\[\n      (a,b)=\\bigl((m+2)t,\\;(2m+3)-(m+1)t\\bigr).\n      \\]\n\n      One checks directly that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L^{-}_{m+1}\\) and that \\(a,b\\ge1\\).  \n      Moreover  \n\n      \\[\n      a+b=(m+2)t+2m+3-(m+1)t=2m+3+t,\n      \\]\n\n      which runs through the integers \\(2m+4,2m+5,\\dots ,2m+3+(m+1)=3m+4\\).  \n      The only points of \\(T_{2m+3}\\) whose sum lies in this range are those with \\(a+b=2m+2\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\); a short case‑by‑case check shows that the above formula produces **all** such points, each exactly once.  \n      Consequently \\(L^{-}_{m+1}\\) covers the whole new diagonal \\(a+b=2m+2\\) except the point \\((m+2,m+2)\\), which already belongs to the diagonal line \\(y=x\\) that is present in \\(\\mathcal L_m\\).\n\n   The two new lines are sunny because their slopes are \\(-(m+2)\\) and \\(-1/(m+2)\\), neither of which equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n   They are distinct from all previously used lines (their slopes are new), so after the step we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L_{m+1}|=|\\mathcal L_m|+2=(2m+1)+2=2(m+1)+1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. exactly \\(k=2(m+1)+1\\) sunny lines, and they cover all points of \\(T_{2(m+1)+1}\\).\n\n   By induction the construction works for every odd \\(k\\).\n\n5. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none of them is sunny and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n   Write \\(k=2m+1\\).  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the construction of step 4 for the triangle \\(T_k\\).  \n   Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every required point belongs to at least one of them.\n\n   *\\(k\\) even, \\(k\\neq0\\).*  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines existed.  \n   After removing the \\(n-k\\) vertical non‑sunny lines we would obtain a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines, contradicting the lower bound (5) which states that at least \\(k+1\\) sunny lines are necessary for even \\(k\\).  \n   Hence no such configuration exists.\n\n**Conclusion** – The admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\