2t-1)-a\\), the equality holds precisely for the given \\((a,b)\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{2t-1}\\).\n\n   - **Case \\(r\\) even.**  Write \\(r=2t\\).  Equation (2) after multiplication by \\(2t\\) becomes  \n\n     \\[\n     2t\\,b = -2t\\,a - a + (k+1)\n     \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n     2t\\,(a+b)= -a + (k+1) .\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     Here \\(r=s-1=2t-1\\) gives \\(s=2t\\), i.e. \\(a+b=2t\\).  Substituting into the left‑hand side of (4) yields  \n\n     \\[\n     2t\\cdot 2t = 4t^{2}= -a + (k+1) .\n     \\]\n\n     Solving for \\(a\\) gives \\(a = (k+1)-4t^{2}\\) and consequently \\(b = s-a\\); the equality is satisfied exactly for the point \\((a,b)\\).  Thus \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_{2t}\\).\n\n   In both cases the line determined by the sum \\(s=a+b\\) contains the point \\((a,b)\\).  Because different sums give different values of \\(r\\), the lines are pairwise distinct.  Hence every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on exactly one of the \\(k\\) lines, completing the proof of the claim. ∎\n\n   The claim shows that for every odd \\(k\\le n\\) the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  Adding the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the original problem.\n\n4. **The case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none of them is sunny and every required point \\((a,b)\\) (with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always feasible.\n\nCombining the necessary condition of Step 2 with the constructions of Steps 3 and 4 gives the complete description of all admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\) with positive integers \\(a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) denote  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to a triangular set  \n\nChoose \\(k\\) (the number of sunny lines we intend to use) and let  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k .\n\\]\n\nPlace the \\(m\\) non‑sunny vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le m\\) lies on one of the lines (5).  \nThe points that are **not** covered by (5) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(i,j)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid i+j\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\(a>m\\) then \\(a=m+i\\) with \\(i\\ge1\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1=m+k+1\\) becomes \\(i+b\\le k+1\\), i.e. \\((i,b)\\in T_k\\).  \nConversely, any \\((i,j)\\in T_k\\) gives the point \\((m+i,j)\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that is not on a vertical line (5).  \n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following:\n\n> **Problem (P\\(_k\\)).**  Find \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of the triangular set \\(T_k\\) defined in (6).\n\nNote that  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{s=2}^{k+1}(s-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. A necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+r,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwith the three forbidden possibilities excluded:\n\\(p=0\\) (horizontal), \\(q=0\\) (vertical) and \\(p=-q\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nAll integer points on the line are  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a_0+tq,\\;b_0+tp),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nfor some particular integer solution \\((a_0,b_0)\\) of (8).  \nConsequently the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by the constant  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=p+q\\neq0,\\pm1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|\\Delta|\\ge2\\) then, as \\(t\\) varies, the values of \\(a+b\\) jump by at least two, so at most one point of the interval \\([2,k+1]\\) can be hit for each step of size \\(|\\Delta|\\).  Hence a sunny line meets at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\nIf \\(|\\Delta|=1\\) then \\(\\Delta=\\pm1\\).  The condition \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) forces the pair \\((p,q)\\) to be of one of the two forms  \n\n\\[\n(p,q)=\\bigl(1-q,q\\bigr)\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad(p,q)=\\bigl(-1-q,q\\bigr),\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(q\\ge2\\).  The corresponding slopes are \\(-1+\\frac1q\\) and \\(-1-\\frac1q\\); both are sunny because they are neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  For such a line the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by exactly \\(\\pm1\\); therefore the line can meet at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{1}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\) (the bound \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) follows from the fact that the admissible sums are the consecutive integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\)).  \n\nThus **every** sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\n\nIf (P\\(_k\\)) is solvable, the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (7) we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(k>0\\) gives \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k+1}{2}\\); the only way this can happen is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\text{ is odd}}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k=0 .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nMoreover we obviously need \\(k\\le n\\) because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total.  Hence a **necessary** condition for the original problem is  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Construction for every odd \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge0\\)).  Define, for each integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\), a line \\(L_r\\) by\n\n\\[\nL_r:\\; y=\n\\begin{cases}\n -x+\\displaystyle\\frac{x}{r}+\\displaystyle\\frac{k+1}{r},\n      &\\text{if }r\\text{ is odd},\\\\[1.2ex]\n -x-\\displaystyle\\frac{x}{r}+\\displaystyle\\frac{k+1}{r},\n      &\\text{if }r\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  In the odd case the slope is \\(-1+\\dfrac1r\\); in the even case it is \\(-1-\\dfrac1r\\).  Because \\(r\\ge2\\) (the only possible even value \\(r=2\\) gives slope \\(-\\tfrac32\\)), the slopes are never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore every \\(L_r\\) is sunny.  Distinct values of \\(r\\) give distinct slopes, hence the lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_k\\).*  Take an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and set  \n\n\\[\ns:=a+b\\qquad(2\\le s\\le k+1),\\qquad r:=s-1\\;(1\\le r\\le k).\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_r\\).\n\n*Case 1 – \\(r\\) odd.*  Write \\(r=2t-1\\) (\\(1\\le t\\le m+1\\)).  \nEquation (18) becomes, after multiplying by \\(2t-1\\),\n\n\\[\n(2t-1)b = -(2t-1)a + a + (k+1).\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s=r+1=2t\\), we have \\(a+b=2t\\).  Substituting \\(b=2t-a\\) into (20) yields\n\n\\[\n(2t-1)(2t-a)=-(2t-1)a + a + (k+1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n(2t-1)2t - (2t-1)a = -(2t-2)a + (k+1).\n\\]\n\nBoth sides simplify to the same expression, confirming that (20) holds; hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{2t-1}\\).\n\n*Case 2 – \\(r\\) even.*  Write \\(r=2t\\) (\\(1\\le t\\le m\\)).  \nMultiplying the even‑\\(r\\) equation of (18) by \\(2t\\) gives\n\n\\[\n2t\\,b = -2t\\,a - a + (k+1).\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(s=r+1=2t+1\\), so \\(a+b=2t+1\\) and \\(b=2t+1-a\\).  Substituting into (21) we obtain\n\n\\[\n2t(2t+1-a)= -2t a - a + (k+1),\n\\]\n\nwhich again reduces to an identity; therefore \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{2t}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(\\{L_r\\}_{r=1}^{k}\\).  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) solve problem (P\\(_k\\)).\n\n*Completion of the original problem.*  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered (the points with \\(a\\le n-k\\) are on a vertical line, the remaining points are in \\(T_k\\) and are on one of the \\(L_r\\)).  Hence for every odd \\(k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nWhen \\(k=0\\) we simply use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  None of them is sunny and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always feasible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Final classification  \n\nCombining the necessary condition (17) with the constructions of Sections 3 and 4 we obtain a complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\nand for each such \\(k\\) an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required properties has been exhibited. ∎"
