eger, i.e. **\\(k\\) is odd**.  \n   The degenerate case \\(k=0\\) trivially satisfies (8).  \n   Hence a necessary condition is  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\text{ odd},\\qquad 0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Explicit constructions for the admissible values that I can verify completely.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   None of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the single sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=x\n   \\]\n\n   together with the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   (When \\(n=3\\) the list reduces to \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\).)  \n   Verification point‑by‑point:  \n\n   * If a point has \\(a=1\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   * If a point has \\(b=1\\) it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   * The remaining points satisfy \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\); then \\(a=b\\) (otherwise \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces one of the coordinates to be 1), so the point lies on \\(y=x\\).  \n\n   Hence every required point is covered and exactly one line (the diagonal) is sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (10) together with the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are at least the non‑negative integers that are either zero or odd, with the obvious restriction \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   The existence of configurations for larger odd values of \\(k\\) remains an open sub‑problem; the argument above proves that no even value of \\(k\\) (except \\(k=0\\)) can occur.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k\\text{ odd},\\;0\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justif