) changes by  \n> \\[\n> \\Delta =p+q\\neq0\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]  \n> when \\(t\\) is increased by one: \\(s(t)=s(0)+t\\Delta\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The parametrisation (3) is correct for a line with rational slope \\(p/q\\) in reduced form.  \n- The change in the sum is indeed \\(\\Delta=p+q\\). Since the slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(p=-q\\)) is excluded, \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\).  \n- No issue here.\n\n**Quoted text (interval length):**  \n> “Because the points of \\(T_k\\) satisfy \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\), the values of \\(t\\) for which a point of the line lies in \\(T_k\\) form an interval of length at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The sum \\(s\\) can vary from a minimum of at least 2 to a maximum of at most \\(k+1\\); the total spread is \\(k-1\\).  \n- If the step size in the sum is \\(|\\Delta|\\), the **number** of integer values of \\(t\\) that keep \\(s\\) inside the interval is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{|\\Delta|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\) (the “+1’’ accounts for the endpoint).  \n- The solution omits this “+1’’ and therefore underestimates the possible length. This off‑by‑one error is not addressed.\n\n**Conclusion:** **Justification Gap** – the interval length is misstated and the reasoning is incomplete.\n\n**Quoted text (second bound):**  \n> “Moreover, each step changes the two coordinates by at least \\(|q|\\) and \\(|p|\\) respectively, both at least 1.  \n> If we move \\(t\\) steps in the direction that increases the sum, then both coordinates increase by at least \\(t\\).  \n> Since for any point of \\(T_k\\) we have \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), we obtain  \n> \\[\n> 2t\\le k-1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\tag{6}\n> \\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The argument that each step increases each coordinate by at least 1 is correct because \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\).  \n- Starting from the smallest admissible point on the line, moving \\(t\\) steps forward indeed forces the sum to increase by at least \\(2t\\).  \n- Hence the bound \\(t\\le\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) is valid for the *forward* direction.  \n- The solution does not discuss the backward direction (negative \\(t\\)), but later it claims to combine (5) and (6) to obtain the final bound.\n\n**Conclusion:** The bound (6) itself is correct, but its use in the final combination is not fully justified (see next item).\n\n**Quoted text (combination):**  \n> “Combining (5) and (6) gives the universal bound  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\text{Every sunny line contains at most }\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points of }T_k.}\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The solution does not explain how the two separate bounds (5) and (6) are merged.  \n- A rigorous argument would note that the number of admissible integer \\(t\\) values is limited simultaneously by the sum‑range bound (5) and by the coordinate‑growth bound (6). The maximum possible number of points occurs when \\(|\\Delta|=2\\) (i.e. \\(p=q=1\\)), giving exactly \\(\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points.  \n- However, because (5) was already misstated (missing “+1’’) and because the interaction between the two bounds is not demonstrated, the derivation of (7) is incomplete.\n\n**Conclusion:** **Justification Gap** – the final bound is asserted without a rigorous combination of the preceding estimates.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 – Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “If the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover all \\(|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points, then by (7)  \n> \\[\n> k\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]  \n> … Dividing by the positive integer \\(k\\) yields  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k+1}{2}.\n> \\tag{9}\n> \\]  \n> Equality in (9) holds **iff** \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) is an integer, i.e. **\\(k\\) is odd**. …”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- Assuming the bound (7) is correct, the inequality (8) follows directly.  \n- Since \\(\\lfloor x\\rfloor\\le x\\) for any real \\(x\\), the only way to have \\(\\lfloor x\\rfloor\\ge x\\) is when \\(x\\) is an integer. Hence the conclusion that \\(k\\) must be odd (or \\(k=0\\)) is valid.  \n- The logical steps are sound, provided (7) holds.\n\n**Conclusion:** This part is **correct** (no error detected).\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 – Explicit constructions  \n\n#### (a) \\(k=0\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). … each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- As noted earlier, for any such point we have \\(a\\le n\\), so the point indeed lies on one of the vertical lines.  \n- All lines are vertical, hence non‑sunny.  \n- No error.\n\n**Conclusion:** Construction for \\(k=0\\) is correct.\n\n#### (b) \\(k=1\\)\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “Use the single sunny line \\(L:\\;y=x\\) together with the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-2 .\n> \\]  \n> … *If a point has \\(a=1\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n> *If a point has \\(b=1\\) it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n> *The remaining points satisfy \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\); then \\(a=b\\) (otherwise \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces one of the coordinates to be 1), so the point lies on \\(y=x\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The claim that every point with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) must satisfy \\(a=b\\) is false. Counterexample: for \\(n=4\\) the point \\((2,3)\\) has \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b=5\\le n+1=5\\) but \\(a\\neq b\\).  \n- The construction provides horizontal lines only up to \\(y=n-2\\). Points with \\(b=n-1\\) (e.g. \\((2,n-1)\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)) are not covered by any horizontal line, nor by \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=x\\). Hence the configuration fails for all \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n- Even for \\(n=3\\) the construction works, but the solution claims it works for *every* \\(n\\ge3\\). The statement is therefore incorrect.\n\n**Conclusion:** **Critical Error** – the construction for \\(k=1\\) is invalid for \\(n\\ge4\\); the claim that \\(k=1\\) is always achievable is false.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 – Conclusion and completeness  \n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “Thus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are at least the non‑negative integers that are either zero or odd, … The existence of configurations for larger odd values of \\(k\\) remains an open sub‑problem …”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The original problem asks for **all** non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration exists.  \n- The solution does **not** provide constructions for odd \\(k>1\\); it merely states that the question is open. Consequently the answer is incomplete and does not solve the problem.  \n- Since the solution itself admits that it does not determine the full set of admissible \\(k\\), this constitutes a **critical error** with respect to the problem’s requirement.\n\n**Conclusion:** **Critical Error** – the solution fails to determine all admissible \\(k\\); the answer is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:**  \nThe solution correctly derives a necessary condition (odd \\(k\\) or \\(k=0\\)) but contains several justification gaps in the reduction and bound arguments, and it makes a critical error in the construction for \\(k=1\\) (the construction does not work for \\(n\\ge4\\)). Moreover, it does not provide configurations for odd \\(k>1\\), leaving the problem unsolved. Hence the solution is invalid."
